- mypy
- ruff
- bandit
- Trivy
- local access to the staged `REQ-001` source tree for canonical enum reuse when available:
  - `runs/kit/REQ-001/src/src`

Logical reuse dependencies from earlier plan artifacts:

- canonical role vocabulary and shared model enum family from `REQ-001`
- shared audit integration seam is reused contractually by passing `ActorContext.user_id` into downstream audit helpers; no new audit module is emitted in this REQ

## Runtime Profiles
Supported profiles in this staging package:

- `local`
  - identity adapter: `HmacJwtIdentityProvider`
  - intended use: developer workstation and repeatable local tests
  - token style: signed HS256 bearer JWT
- `aws`
  - identity adapter: `TrustedHeaderIdentityProvider` or a drop-in enterprise verifier implementing the same interface
  - intended use: ECS/Fargate behind OIDC/SAML-capable ingress or identity proxy
  - claim source: upstream-verified claims passed to the app
- `onprem`
  - identity adapter: `TrustedHeaderIdentityProvider` or a drop-in enterprise verifier implementing the same interface
  - intended use: reverse proxy / gateway with SAML or OIDC claim forwarding
  - claim source: upstream-verified claims passed to the app

What stays identical across profiles:

- `ActorContext`
- claim-to-role mapping rules
- RBAC role vocabulary
- authorization deny semantics
- actor propagation into downstream state-changing handlers

What changes across profiles:

- how raw identity claims are obtained before mapping into `ActorContext`

## Pre Requirements Setup
From repository root:

```bash
python --version
mkdir -p runs/kit/REQ-002/test/reports
export PYTHONPATH="runs/kit/REQ-002/src/src:runs/kit/REQ-001/src/src:${PYTHONPATH}"
```

Install required tools in the active environment if they are not already present:

```bash
python -m pip install fastapi pytest pytest-cov mypy ruff bandit
```

Trivy is expected as an external binary for the security gate.

## Local Development Mode
Local mode uses the same identity contract as target runtime, but the raw claim source is a signed bearer JWT.

Recommended local settings:

```bash
export AUTH_JWT_SHARED_SECRET="change-me-local"
export AUTH_JWT_ISSUER="https://local-idp.example"
export AUTH_JWT_AUDIENCE="docufu-api"
```

Use `HmacJwtIdentityProvider` in local app wiring. The mapper still expects the same business claims:

- `sub` or SAML name-id equivalent
- a role/group claim that resolves to `operator`, `admin`, or `auditor`
- optional `user_id`, `email`, and MFA claims

This keeps the business-facing auth contract stable while allowing fully local tests without introducing local password auth.

## Target Runtime Mode
Target runtime mode is aligned to enterprise OIDC/SAML deployment:

- app receives already verified claims from ingress, ALB/auth proxy, or enterprise gateway
- app maps those claims into `ActorContext`
- downstream business modules consume only the mapped actor and RBAC decisions

The emitted `TrustedHeaderIdentityProvider` is the production-close seam for this REQ. It is designed so later promotion can wire a gateway-backed or JWKS-backed verifier without changing:

- route dependencies
- actor model
- RBAC matrix
- downstream audit usage

Expected target-mode claim transport:

- `X-Auth-Claims` header containing base64url JSON claims from a trusted upstream verifier

